  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "Source-tool tests publish OutlineId addresses and retain mapped NodeIds\n directly in dependencies. This required Domain method is unused in\n those flows; stable-path and filesystem-storage tests use other domains."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/project_sources.rs` `mod[tests]/impl[Project/Domain]/fn[node_path]` is valid.\n\n- **Form:** It is a fenced citation with commentary on an explicit function that has a body. The built-in allows this and excuses only that function's zero execution.\n- **Substance:** The reason is a specific claim about which code runs, not just difficulty. The source-tool tests publish `OutlineId` addresses and keep mapped `NodeId`s directly in dependencies, so they never need a node path.\n- **No callers in these tests:** I searched the crate for `node_path` calls and for uses without a call. Non-test callers are only in `agent/tools.rs` and `graph.rs`.\n  - In `agent/tools.rs` (`ToolAgent` `run`, `GraphSession::node` and `dispatch`), the callers belong to the graph-selection tools. The `project_sources.rs` tests never use `ToolAgent` or any graph tools.\n  - In `graph.rs`, `edge_fingerprint` is the only caller. It runs only for `Dependency::OutgoingEdges`. The `Project` fixture's `outgoing_edges` always returns an empty list, so the closure that calls `node_path` never executes. The fixture's own `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` ignore the node.\n- **Other domains:** The commentary says stable-path and filesystem-storage tests use other domains. That matches the code. `tests/contracts.rs` (`Application`, `Relationships`) and `tests/storage.rs` with `tests/support/stored_graph.rs` (`StoredGraph`) each define their own `node_path`. `graph/validation_tests.rs` (`ReviewDomain`) does the same.\n- **Consistency:** The explanation agrees with the current source. I did not check measured coverage, so I assumed the function has recorded zero execution.",
